.TH APPATTIC 1 "2026-11-13" "appattic 3.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
appattic \- find leftover data, unused software, outdated packages, and disk usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B appattic
.RB [ --version ]
.RB [ --help ]
.RI [ command ]
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B appattic help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B appattic disk
.RI [ PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B appattic
scans the machine for data left behind by uninstalled apps, installed software
with little recorded usage, unused distro and language packages, packages with
a newer version, and folder sizes.
.B appattic update
runs named package upgrades after confirmation.
.B erase
deletes the scan snapshot, and
.B restore
replaces settings from a backup. Reporting commands may refresh the local
scan cache; they do not remove software or leftover data.
.PP
With no command it prints the full report. A scan is cached and reused for 24
hours while its fingerprint remains current; the status line on standard
error names the scan time and points at
.BR \-\-fresh .
.PP
Progress and status go to standard error. On a terminal, reports and
.B \-\-dry\-run
scripts go to standard output. When standard output is not a terminal, it
carries the same JSON document
.B \-\-json
writes, unless
.B \-o text
is set.
.B appattic -o text leftovers --top 10 | head
keeps the report as text while status stays on standard error.
In JSON mode, a
.B \-\-dry\-run
script goes to standard error; use
.B \-o text
to capture the script on standard output.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B config
Print effective settings, state-file paths, resolved XDG roots, and relevant
environment values. Nothing is scanned. Output includes full ignored paths
and environment values; review it before sharing.
.TP
.B report
Leftovers, stale software, outdated packages, and packages. The default.
.TP
.B leftovers
Only orphaned data and PATH overlays from uninstalled apps.
.TP
.B stale
Unused installed software, tiered KEEP, REVIEW, and REMOVE.
.TP
.B outdated
Installed packages with a newer version available.
.TP
.B packages
Distro orphans and language globals.
.TP
.BI "disk " [PATH ]
Folder sizes as a tree. PATH defaults to the home directory. Prefix a bare
folder name with
.B ./
to distinguish it from a command. Use
.B \-\-
before a path that starts with a dash.
.TP
.B erase
Delete the stored scan snapshot without prompting. Settings and their backups
are kept. This works even when settings cannot be parsed.
.TP
.B restore
Validate
.I settings.json.bak
and restore it as
.IR settings.json .
Readable, nonempty differing current contents are kept in
.IR settings.json.bad .
An invalid or missing backup exits 1 without changing files. Current settings
do not have to parse. Before repeating a restore, copy any existing
.I settings.json.bad
elsewhere if you need to keep it.
.TP
.B schema
Print the clispec v0.3 document and exit. A command name after it keeps that
command. The document is JSON even when
.B \-o text
is set, and the command reads no settings.
.TP
.BI "update " [ \-\-yes ]
Upgrade the named packages the scan found. It prompts on a terminal and exits
2 without one unless
.B \-\-yes
is given. Always scans live. A failed or timed-out script may have applied
some upgrades; rescan before retrying.
.PP
.B \-\-
ends the options, so a
.B disk
root that starts with a dash is passed as given:
.B appattic disk -- -backup
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " MODE
.BR auto ", " text ", or " json .
.B auto
is the default: text on a terminal, and the JSON document
.B \-\-json
would write when standard output is not a terminal.
.B \-o text
keeps the text report when piped. The flag may come before the command.
.TP
.BI \-\-json " FILE"
Also write this command's result to FILE, which has to end in
.BR .json .
The report commands, and
.BR update ,
write the whole scan;
.B disk
writes the disk tree, and
.BR config ,
.B erase
and
.B restore
write what those commands reported.
.BR \-\-top ,
.BR \-\-category ,
.B \-\-leftovers\-only
and
.B \-\-stale\-only
shape text reports, not JSON output. Other filename extensions are usage errors.
.TP
.B \-\-include\-system
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, update, and config: include OS
system apps in the stale list. It turns the setting on for this run and cannot
turn it off when the settings file already has it true.
.TP
.B \-\-fresh
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, and update: ignore the
last-scan cache and scan now.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
On report, leftovers, stale, outdated, packages, and update: print the shell
script for the command without running it.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
On report and leftovers: only the N largest. On disk: the N largest entries per
folder.
.TP
.BI \-\-category " CAT"
On report and leftovers: filter by category substring. Repeat for multiple
categories. A command name is not accepted as the value.
.TP
.B \-\-leftovers\-only
On report: skip stale, outdated, and packages.
.TP
.B \-\-stale\-only
On report: skip leftovers, outdated, and packages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI color.
.B NO_COLOR
set to a non-empty value and
.B TERM=dumb
do the same.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-file\-systems
On disk: descend into other mounted devices.
.TP
.B \-\-allocated
On disk: print allocated blocks instead of apparent size.
.TP
.BR \-\-yes ", " \-y
On update: skip the prompt. Required without a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print help and exit.
.B appattic help
is equivalent; an optional command name is validated.
.PP
Command-specific options are rejected on other commands. For example,
.B appattic stale --top 5
exits 2.
.BR \-\-help " and " \-\-version
take precedence over usage errors.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The run failed, or an update was cancelled.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: a bad command, option, or value; a disk root that is missing or is
not a directory; an update with no terminal and no
.BR \-\-yes ;
or a malformed
.IR settings.json .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A non-empty value disables color, as
.B \-\-no\-color
does.
.TP
.B TERM
.B dumb
disables color.
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
.IR fg;bg .
The background decides the dark or light status colors.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Absolute data root, the parent of
.I appattic/settings.json
and
.IR appattic/last-scan.json .
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " XDG_STATE_HOME
Absolute configuration, cache, and state roots, scanned for leftovers.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_DIRS
Colon-separated absolute data roots searched for desktop entries. Unset, empty,
or a list whose entries are all relative uses
.IR /usr/local/share:/usr/share ;
relative entries in a longer list are dropped, and
.B appattic config
prints the list that survives.
.TP
.BR ANDROID_HOME ", " ANDROID_SDK_ROOT
An Android SDK root the scan treats as a user tool directory. The first of the
two that holds a real SDK is the root in force; a value that names no such
directory is read as unset, and the fixed default roots are searched instead.
.B appattic config
reports which root is in force and whether an exported value was ignored.
.TP
.B APPATTIC_PAGE
Read by the GUI window, not by this command; listed because
.B appattic config
reports it with every other switch a Linux run's window reads. The sidebar
page the window opens on:
.BR overview ,
.BR leftovers ,
.BR stale ,
.BR outdated ,
.BR packages ,
.BR disk ,
or
.BR settings .
An unset or empty value opens the overview; an unknown name is reported on
stderr and also opens the overview. See
.BR appattic\-qt (1).
.TP
.B LANG
The locale whose
.I .lproj
directory the macOS app-name lookup reads for an app's localized name. Unset,
empty, or a value that is only an encoding opens the base English names.
.B appattic config
prints the locale it resolved to.
.PP
Per the XDG Base Directory specification, an empty or relative XDG path is
ignored and the standard user default is used. The accepted values and their
effects are the ones
.B appattic config
prints.
.SH FILES
.PP
State files share one directory:
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/appattic
(default
.IR ~/.local/share/appattic )
on Linux and
.I ~/Library/Application Support/AppAttic
on macOS. Generated scripts use temporary files outside this directory.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json
.IR includeSystem ", " confirmDelete ", and the ignored leftover paths, which
must each be the full path a report prints. A missing file uses the defaults. A
malformed file is an error, and the run exits 2 without overwriting it.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json.bak
Previous differing settings bytes, when backup creation succeeded, kept so
an emptied or mangled
.I settings.json
can be put back with
.B appattic restore
or, by hand, with a
.BR "cp" .
It is not loaded as active settings.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/settings.json.bad
Readable, nonempty differing settings bytes preserved before a restore.
A later restore can replace this copy; copy it elsewhere first if needed.
It is not loaded as active settings, and
.B appattic erase
leaves it. See
.IR docs/runbooks/state-recovery.md .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/appattic/last-scan.json
The owner-only scan cache, including discovered app and leftover paths. A run that updates packages drops
it, so does
.BR "appattic erase" ,
and so does a run more than 24 hours after the one that wrote it. It may also be discarded earlier when the
machine fingerprint changes or the snapshot cannot be decoded.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ten largest leftovers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic leftovers --top 10
.fi
.RE
.PP
Export the whole scan and keep the report on standard output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic -o text report --json "$HOME/scan.json" > report.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
The ten largest folders under /var, by allocated blocks:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic disk /var --top 10 --allocated
.fi
.RE
.PP
Filter by two categories:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic leftovers --category caches --category browser
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what an update would run, then run it unattended:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic update --dry-run
appattic update --yes
.fi
.RE
.PP
Put a settings file back after it was emptied, truncated, or hand-mangled:
.PP
.RS
.nf
appattic restore
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
The project README and
.UR https://github.com/maci0/appattic
.UE
for the full scan description, and
.BR appattic\-qt (1)
for the Linux window.
